-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- X01 · Business Acumen dan Basic Statistics
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
UPDATE public.sessions SET
 content_en = E'## Why business acumen comes first\n\nA dashboard full of charts is worthless if it answers the wrong question. **Business acumen** is what tells you which question matters. Before touching a single formula, a strong analyst understands how the business makes money, where it loses money, and which levers move the needle.\n\nThroughout this program we follow **TokoSegar**, a fresh-grocery retail chain with 40 outlets and a growing online store. Every concept ties back to a real TokoSegar decision.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What business acumen actually means\n\nBusiness acumen is the ability to read a business through three lenses at once:\n\n| Lens | The question it asks | TokoSegar example |\n|------|----------------------|-------------------|\n| **Revenue** | How do we earn, and from whom? | In-store baskets + online orders + a loyalty subscription |\n| **Cost** | What does it cost to earn that? | Rent, staff, spoilage of fresh produce, delivery |\n| **Customer** | Who buys, how often, why do they leave? | Weekly families vs one-off online shoppers |\n\nAn analyst without acumen reports *"online sales fell 8% in March."* An analyst with acumen reports *"online sales fell 8% because delivery delays pushed our weekly families back to in-store — and those families are 3× more valuable."* Same data, very different value.\n\n> **Rule of thumb:** every number you report should connect to money, customers, or risk. If it connects to none of those, ask why you are reporting it.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Mapping a business process\n\nEvery business is a chain of **input → process → output**. Mapping it shows you *where* data is created and *what* is worth measuring.\n\n`Raw materials & orders → Store operations → Fulfilled sales`\n\nA closer look at TokoSegar''s online order:\n\n| Stage | What happens | Data created | Candidate metric |\n|-------|--------------|--------------|------------------|\n| Input | Customer places order | order_id, items, timestamp | Orders per day |\n| Process | Pick, pack, dispatch | pick time, packer_id | Avg fulfilment time |\n| Output | Delivered to customer | delivered_at, rating | On-time delivery % |\n\n### Leading vs lagging indicators\n\nOnce you can see the process, you can pick metrics that **predict** the future (leading) versus ones that **report** the past (lagging).\n\n| Type | Definition | Examples | Use it to… |\n|------|------------|----------|-----------|\n| **Leading** | Moves *before* the outcome | New leads, cart adds, avg fulfilment time | Steer while there is still time to act |\n| **Lagging** | Confirms *after* the outcome | Revenue, churn, net profit | Judge whether a past decision worked |\n\n> **Mini case study — the delivery alarm.** TokoSegar''s revenue (lagging) looked fine in week 1, but average fulfilment time (leading) crept from 20 to 34 minutes. Because the team watched the leading metric, they added a packer *before* the revenue dropped. Lagging-only teams would have seen it a month too late.\n\n---\n\n## 3. From a vague question to a sharp problem statement\n\nStakeholders ask vague questions. Your first job is to sharpen them.\n\n| Vague ask | Sharpened problem statement |\n|-----------|------------------------------|\n| "Sales are bad." | "In-store basket size fell 12% MoM in the Jakarta region in March 2026 — which categories drove the drop?" |\n| "Are customers happy?" | "Has the 30-day repeat-purchase rate for online customers changed since the April delivery-fee increase?" |\n| "Should we open a new store?" | "Do the 3 candidate districts have enough weekly-grocery demand to reach break-even within 12 months?" |\n\n### The 5W1H frame\n\n**What** changed, **Where**, **When**, **Who** is affected, **Why** it matters, **How** we will measure it. A statement that answers all six is ready to analyse.\n\n### The 5 Whys (find the real driver)\n\n```\nBasket size fell\n↓ why? → fewer items per order\n↓ why? → fresh produce not added\n↓ why? → produce shelves looked empty by afternoon\n↓ why? → restock happens once, in the morning\n↓ why? → staff roster has no midday restock\n```\n\nThe fifth *why* — the roster — is the fixable root cause. Stopping at "fewer items" would have hidden it.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Descriptive statistics that matter\n\nSummary statistics compress thousands of rows into a few honest numbers. Consider TokoSegar''s basket totals (in thousand Rupiah) for one afternoon:\n\n`45, 52, 48, 51, 47, 300, 49`\n\n| Measure | Excel formula | Value | What it tells you |\n|---------|---------------|-------|-------------------|\n| Mean | `=AVERAGE(A2:A8)` | 84.6 | The average — but distorted by the 300 outlier |\n| Median | `=MEDIAN(A2:A8)` | 49 | The middle value — the *typical* basket |\n| Mode | `=MODE.SNGL(A2:A8)` | — | The most frequent value |\n| Range | `=MAX-MIN` | 255 | Spread from smallest to largest |\n| Std dev | `=STDEV.S(A2:A8)` | 94.7 | How far values sit from the mean |\n\nNotice the mean (84.6) describes *no actual customer*. The median (49) is the honest "typical basket". That gap is the outlier''s fingerprint.\n\n### Which measure, when?\n\n| Situation | Use | Why |\n|-----------|-----|-----|\n| Data has outliers (income, basket size) | **Median** | Resistant to extreme values |\n| Symmetric data, no extremes | **Mean** | Uses every value |\n| Categorical / most-common | **Mode** | Only measure that works on labels |\n| Describing consistency/risk | **Std dev + range** | Two shops can share a mean but differ wildly |\n\n> **Mini case study — two stores, same average.** Store A and Store B both average Rp 50k baskets. Store A''s std dev is 5; Store B''s is 40. Store B has both whales and tiny baskets — a segmentation opportunity the average completely hid.\n\n---\n\n## Common pitfalls & edge cases\n\n- **Reporting the mean on skewed data.** One corporate bulk order makes "average basket" meaningless — quote the median too.\n- **Confusing correlation with cause.** Ice-cream sales and fan sales both rise in summer; neither causes the other.\n- **Vanity metrics.** Total page views feels good but does not connect to money — prefer conversion rate.\n- **Std dev of tiny samples.** With 3 data points, std dev is unstable; note your sample size.\n\n---\n\n## Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| Metric | A number that tracks something the business cares about |\n| Leading indicator | Predicts an outcome before it happens |\n| Lagging indicator | Confirms an outcome after it happened |\n| Problem statement | A specific, measurable framing of the question |\n| Outlier | A value far from the rest of the data |\n| Standard deviation | Average distance of values from the mean (spread) |\n\n---\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. Business acumen decides *which* question is worth answering — always tie a number to money, customers, or risk.\n2. Map the **input → process → output** chain to see where data is born and what to measure.\n3. **Leading** metrics let you steer; **lagging** metrics let you judge. Watch both.\n4. Sharpen vague asks with **5W1H**; find root causes with the **5 Whys**.\n5. On skewed data, the **median** is more honest than the mean; **std dev** reveals risk the average hides.\n\n> 💡 Practice the concepts with the matching exercises below, then build the business-process & metrics worksheet in the assignment.',
 content_id = E'## Kenapa business acumen didahulukan\n\nDashboard penuh grafik tidak berguna kalau menjawab pertanyaan yang salah. **Business acumen** adalah yang memberi tahu pertanyaan mana yang penting. Sebelum menyentuh satu rumus pun, analyst yang kuat memahami bagaimana bisnis menghasilkan uang, di mana kehilangan uang, dan tuas mana yang menggerakkan hasil.\n\nSepanjang program ini kita mengikuti **TokoSegar**, jaringan ritel grocery segar dengan 40 outlet dan toko online yang berkembang. Setiap konsep dikaitkan ke keputusan nyata TokoSegar.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Apa arti business acumen sebenarnya\n\nBusiness acumen adalah kemampuan membaca bisnis lewat tiga lensa sekaligus:\n\n| Lensa | Pertanyaannya | Contoh TokoSegar |\n|-------|---------------|------------------|\n| **Revenue** | Bagaimana kita menghasilkan, dari siapa? | Basket di toko + order online + langganan loyalty |\n| **Cost** | Berapa biayanya untuk menghasilkan itu? | Sewa, staf, susut produk segar, pengiriman |\n| **Customer** | Siapa yang beli, seberapa sering, kenapa pergi? | Keluarga mingguan vs pembeli online sekali beli |\n\nAnalyst tanpa acumen melapor *"penjualan online turun 8% di Maret."* Analyst dengan acumen melapor *"penjualan online turun 8% karena keterlambatan pengiriman mendorong keluarga mingguan kembali ke toko — dan keluarga itu 3× lebih berharga."* Data sama, nilai sangat berbeda.\n\n> **Aturan praktis:** setiap angka yang Anda laporkan harus terhubung ke uang, pelanggan, atau risiko. Kalau tidak terhubung ke salah satunya, tanyakan kenapa Anda melaporkannya.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Memetakan proses bisnis\n\nSetiap bisnis adalah rantai **input → proses → output**. Memetakannya menunjukkan *di mana* data lahir dan *apa* yang layak diukur.\n\n`Bahan baku & order → Operasi toko → Penjualan terpenuhi`\n\nLebih dekat pada order online TokoSegar:\n\n| Tahap | Yang terjadi | Data yang lahir | Kandidat metrik |\n|-------|--------------|-----------------|-----------------|\n| Input | Pelanggan membuat order | order_id, item, timestamp | Order per hari |\n| Proses | Pick, pack, kirim | waktu pick, packer_id | Rata-rata waktu pemenuhan |\n| Output | Sampai ke pelanggan | delivered_at, rating | % pengiriman tepat waktu |\n\n### Leading vs lagging indicator\n\nSetelah proses terlihat, Anda bisa memilih metrik yang **memprediksi** masa depan (leading) versus yang **melaporkan** masa lalu (lagging).\n\n| Tipe | Definisi | Contoh | Gunakan untuk… |\n|------|----------|--------|----------------|\n| **Leading** | Bergerak *sebelum* hasil | Lead baru, add-to-cart, waktu pemenuhan | Mengarahkan selagi masih sempat bertindak |\n| **Lagging** | Mengonfirmasi *setelah* hasil | Revenue, churn, laba bersih | Menilai apakah keputusan lalu berhasil |\n\n> **Studi kasus mini — alarm pengiriman.** Revenue TokoSegar (lagging) tampak baik di minggu 1, tapi rata-rata waktu pemenuhan (leading) merangkak dari 20 ke 34 menit. Karena tim memantau metrik leading, mereka menambah packer *sebelum* revenue turun. Tim yang hanya melihat lagging akan telat sebulan.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Dari pertanyaan samar ke problem statement yang tajam\n\nStakeholder mengajukan pertanyaan samar. Tugas pertama Anda menajamkannya.\n\n| Permintaan samar | Problem statement yang tajam |\n|------------------|------------------------------|\n| "Penjualan jelek." | "Basket size di toko turun 12% MoM di wilayah Jakarta pada Maret 2026 — kategori apa yang mendorong penurunan?" |\n| "Apa pelanggan senang?" | "Apakah repeat-purchase 30 hari pelanggan online berubah sejak kenaikan biaya kirim April?" |\n| "Perlu buka toko baru?" | "Apakah 3 kandidat distrik punya cukup permintaan grocery mingguan untuk BEP dalam 12 bulan?" |\n\n### Kerangka 5W1H\n\n**What** yang berubah, **Where**, **When**, **Who** yang terdampak, **Why** ini penting, **How** cara mengukurnya. Statement yang menjawab keenamnya siap dianalisis.\n\n### 5 Whys (temukan pendorong sebenarnya)\n\n```\nBasket size turun\n↓ kenapa? → item per order berkurang\n↓ kenapa? → produk segar tidak ditambahkan\n↓ kenapa? → rak produk terlihat kosong sore hari\n↓ kenapa? → restock hanya sekali, pagi hari\n↓ kenapa? → roster staf tidak ada restock siang\n```\n\n*Why* kelima — roster — adalah akar masalah yang bisa diperbaiki. Berhenti di "item berkurang" akan menyembunyikannya.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Statistik deskriptif yang penting\n\nStatistik ringkasan memadatkan ribuan baris menjadi beberapa angka jujur. Lihat total basket TokoSegar (ribu Rupiah) satu sore:\n\n`45, 52, 48, 51, 47, 300, 49`\n\n| Ukuran | Rumus Excel | Nilai | Artinya |\n|--------|-------------|-------|---------|\n| Mean | `=AVERAGE(A2:A8)` | 84,6 | Rata-rata — tapi terdistorsi outlier 300 |\n| Median | `=MEDIAN(A2:A8)` | 49 | Nilai tengah — basket *tipikal* |\n| Modus | `=MODE.SNGL(A2:A8)` | — | Nilai paling sering muncul |\n| Range | `=MAX-MIN` | 255 | Rentang dari terkecil ke terbesar |\n| Std dev | `=STDEV.S(A2:A8)` | 94,7 | Seberapa jauh nilai dari mean |\n\nPerhatikan mean (84,6) tidak menggambarkan *pelanggan mana pun*. Median (49) adalah "basket tipikal" yang jujur. Selisih itu adalah sidik jari outlier.\n\n### Ukuran mana, kapan?\n\n| Situasi | Pakai | Kenapa |\n|---------|-------|--------|\n| Ada outlier (pendapatan, basket) | **Median** | Tahan terhadap nilai ekstrem |\n| Data simetris, tanpa ekstrem | **Mean** | Memakai setiap nilai |\n| Kategorikal / paling umum | **Modus** | Satu-satunya untuk label |\n| Menggambarkan konsistensi/risiko | **Std dev + range** | Dua toko bisa ber-mean sama tapi jauh berbeda |\n\n> **Studi kasus mini — dua toko, rata-rata sama.** Toko A dan B sama-sama rata-rata basket Rp 50k. Std dev Toko A 5; Toko B 40. Toko B punya pembeli besar dan sangat kecil — peluang segmentasi yang disembunyikan rata-rata.\n\n---\n\n## Kesalahan umum & edge case\n\n- **Melaporkan mean pada data miring.** Satu order borongan korporat membuat "rata-rata basket" tak bermakna — sertakan median.\n- **Mengira korelasi = sebab.** Penjualan es krim dan kipas sama-sama naik saat panas; tidak saling menyebabkan.\n- **Vanity metric.** Total page view terasa enak tapi tak terhubung uang — pilih conversion rate.\n- **Std dev sampel kecil.** Dengan 3 data, std dev tidak stabil; catat ukuran sampel.\n\n---\n\n## Glosarium\n\n| Istilah | Arti |\n|---------|------|\n| Metrik | Angka yang melacak sesuatu yang bisnis pedulikan |\n| Leading indicator | Memprediksi hasil sebelum terjadi |\n| Lagging indicator | Mengonfirmasi hasil setelah terjadi |\n| Problem statement | Framing pertanyaan yang spesifik dan terukur |\n| Outlier | Nilai yang jauh dari data lain |\n| Standar deviasi | Rata-rata jarak nilai dari mean (sebaran) |\n\n---\n\n## Kesimpulan Utama\n\n1. Business acumen menentukan pertanyaan mana yang layak dijawab — selalu kaitkan angka ke uang, pelanggan, atau risiko.\n2. Petakan rantai **input → proses → output** untuk melihat di mana data lahir dan apa yang diukur.\n3. Metrik **leading** untuk mengarahkan; **lagging** untuk menilai. Pantau keduanya.\n4. Tajamkan permintaan samar dengan **5W1H**; temukan akar masalah dengan **5 Whys**.\n5. Pada data miring, **median** lebih jujur dari mean; **std dev** mengungkap risiko yang disembunyikan rata-rata.\n\n> 💡 Latih konsep lewat exercise matching di bawah, lalu susun kertas kerja proses bisnis & metrik di tugas.'
WHERE session_number = 'X01';

-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- X02 · Membersihkan dan Merapihkan Data
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
UPDATE public.sessions SET
 content_en = E'## Why messy data is the default\n\nReal data arrives dirty: trailing spaces, duplicate rows, blank cells, inconsistent capitalisation, numbers stored as text. Analysts spend up to 60–80% of their time cleaning. Doing it *systematically* — not ad hoc — is the skill.\n\nTokoSegar just exported a customer list from an old POS system. We will clean it together.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding duplicates and blanks\n\n### Duplicates\n- **Data → Remove Duplicates** deletes exact repeats (choose which columns define "duplicate").\n- To *flag* without deleting: `=COUNTIF($A$2:$A$100, A2) > 1` returns TRUE for repeated values.\n\n### Blank cells\n- **Home → Find & Select → Go To Special → Blanks** selects every empty cell at once.\n- Count them: `=COUNTBLANK(B2:B100)`.\n\n| Raw customer_id | phone | Problem |\n|-----------------|-------|---------|\n| C001 | 0812… | ok |\n| C001 | 0812… | duplicate row |\n| C002 | *(blank)* | missing phone |\n\n> Decide *before* deleting: is a duplicate a data error, or a customer who really ordered twice? Deleting blindly destroys real records.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Understanding and fixing errors\n\n| Error | Means | Common cause | Fix |\n|-------|-------|--------------|-----|\n| `#N/A` | Not found | VLOOKUP/XLOOKUP has no match | `=IFNA(formula, "not found")` |\n| `#REF!` | Bad reference | A referenced cell/column was deleted | Rebuild the reference |\n| `#VALUE!` | Wrong type | Text where a number is expected | `VALUE()`, fix the input |\n| `#DIV/0!` | Divide by zero | Denominator is 0 or blank | `=IFERROR(a/b, 0)` |\n| `#NAME?` | Unknown name | Misspelled function or range name | Correct the spelling |\n\n**Two wrappers do most of the work:**\n```\n=IFERROR(VLOOKUP(...), "check manually")   → catches ANY error\n=IFNA(VLOOKUP(...), "no match")            → catches only #N/A\n```\nPrefer `IFNA` when you only want to hide "not found" but still see genuine `#REF!`/`#VALUE!` bugs.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The text-cleanup toolkit\n\n| Function | Does | Example → Result |\n|----------|------|------------------|\n| `TRIM` | Removes extra spaces | `TRIM("  Budi  ")` → `Budi` |\n| `CLEAN` | Strips non-printable chars | `CLEAN(A2)` |\n| `PROPER` | Title-cases text | `PROPER("budi santoso")` → `Budi Santoso` |\n| `UPPER`/`LOWER` | Changes case | `UPPER("inv")` → `INV` |\n| `LEFT`/`RIGHT`/`MID` | Extract parts | `LEFT("INV-2024",3)` → `INV` |\n| `CONCAT` | Join text | `CONCAT(A2," ",B2)` |\n| `VALUE` | Text → number | `VALUE("1500")` → `1500` |\n\n**Before → after on TokoSegar names:**\n\n| Raw | Formula | Clean |\n|-----|---------|-------|\n| `"  budi  "` | `=PROPER(TRIM(A2))` | `Budi` |\n| `"INV-2024-0007"` | `=RIGHT(A2,4)` | `0007` |\n| `"1.500"` (text) | `=VALUE(SUBSTITUTE(A2,".",""))` | `1500` |\n\n---\n\n## 4. A repeatable cleaning workflow\n\n```\nMake a copy of the raw data → never edit the original\n↓\nRemove exact duplicates → decide the key columns first\n↓\nStandardise text → TRIM + PROPER + fix casing\n↓\nFix types → text-numbers, dates\n↓\nHandle blanks & errors → IFERROR/IFNA, or flag for review\n↓\nSpot-check → sort, filter, sanity-check totals\n```\n\n> **Mini case study — the supplier export.** A supplier sent 5,000 rows where prices like `"Rp 12.500"` were text, so `SUM` returned 0. `=VALUE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(A2,"Rp ",""),".",""))` converted them, and the total finally matched the invoice. One formula, an afternoon saved.\n\n---\n\n## Common pitfalls\n\n- **Editing the raw file.** Always clean on a copy; keep the original for audit.\n- **Deleting blanks you did not investigate.** A blank phone ≠ a blank order total.\n- **TRIM does not remove *all* spaces** — non-breaking spaces (char 160) need `SUBSTITUTE(A2,CHAR(160),"")`.\n- **Hiding errors you should fix.** `IFERROR(...,0)` can silently turn a real bug into a wrong zero.\n\n---\n\n## Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| Duplicate | A row that repeats an existing record |\n| Blank / null | A cell with no value |\n| Data type | Whether a value is text, number, or date |\n| Wrapper function | A function like IFERROR that wraps another to handle its errors |\n| Normalisation | Making formats consistent (case, spacing, units) |\n\n---\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. Cleaning is systematic, not ad hoc — always work on a **copy** of the raw data.\n2. Find duplicates with **Remove Duplicates** / `COUNTIF`; find blanks with **Go To Special** / `COUNTBLANK`.\n3. Know the five common errors and wrap formulas with **IFERROR / IFNA** — but do not hide bugs you should fix.\n4. `TRIM`, `PROPER`, `LEFT/RIGHT/MID`, `VALUE` are your everyday cleanup toolkit.\n5. Follow the **copy → dedup → standardise → fix types → handle blanks → spot-check** workflow every time.\n\n> 💡 Try the matching exercises below, then clean the main dataset in the assignment.',
 content_id = E'## Kenapa data berantakan itu normal\n\nData nyata datang kotor: spasi berlebih, baris duplikat, sel kosong, kapitalisasi tidak konsisten, angka tersimpan sebagai teks. Analyst menghabiskan hingga 60–80% waktu untuk membersihkan. Melakukannya *secara sistematis* — bukan asal — itulah keterampilannya.\n\nTokoSegar baru mengekspor daftar pelanggan dari sistem POS lama. Kita bersihkan bersama.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Menemukan duplikat dan sel kosong\n\n### Duplikat\n- **Data → Remove Duplicates** menghapus pengulangan persis (pilih kolom yang mendefinisikan "duplikat").\n- Untuk *menandai* tanpa menghapus: `=COUNTIF($A$2:$A$100, A2) > 1` bernilai TRUE untuk nilai berulang.\n\n### Sel kosong\n- **Home → Find & Select → Go To Special → Blanks** memilih semua sel kosong sekaligus.\n- Hitung: `=COUNTBLANK(B2:B100)`.\n\n| customer_id mentah | phone | Masalah |\n|--------------------|-------|---------|\n| C001 | 0812… | ok |\n| C001 | 0812… | baris duplikat |\n| C002 | *(kosong)* | phone hilang |\n\n> Putuskan *sebelum* menghapus: apakah duplikat itu error data, atau pelanggan yang memang order dua kali? Menghapus membabi buta merusak record asli.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Memahami dan memperbaiki error\n\n| Error | Artinya | Penyebab umum | Perbaikan |\n|-------|---------|---------------|-----------|\n| `#N/A` | Tidak ditemukan | VLOOKUP/XLOOKUP tanpa kecocokan | `=IFNA(rumus, "tidak ada")` |\n| `#REF!` | Referensi rusak | Sel/kolom yang dirujuk dihapus | Bangun ulang referensi |\n| `#VALUE!` | Tipe salah | Teks di tempat angka | `VALUE()`, perbaiki input |\n| `#DIV/0!` | Bagi nol | Penyebut 0 atau kosong | `=IFERROR(a/b, 0)` |\n| `#NAME?` | Nama tak dikenal | Fungsi/nama range salah ketik | Perbaiki ejaan |\n\n**Dua pembungkus menyelesaikan sebagian besar pekerjaan:**\n```\n=IFERROR(VLOOKUP(...), "cek manual")   → menangkap SEMUA error\n=IFNA(VLOOKUP(...), "tidak cocok")      → menangkap hanya #N/A\n```\nUtamakan `IFNA` bila Anda hanya ingin menyembunyikan "tidak ditemukan" tapi tetap melihat bug `#REF!`/`#VALUE!` yang asli.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Perangkat pembersih teks\n\n| Fungsi | Kegunaan | Contoh → Hasil |\n|--------|----------|----------------|\n| `TRIM` | Hapus spasi berlebih | `TRIM("  Budi  ")` → `Budi` |\n| `CLEAN` | Buang karakter non-printable | `CLEAN(A2)` |\n| `PROPER` | Huruf kapital tiap kata | `PROPER("budi santoso")` → `Budi Santoso` |\n| `UPPER`/`LOWER` | Ubah kapitalisasi | `UPPER("inv")` → `INV` |\n| `LEFT`/`RIGHT`/`MID` | Ambil sebagian | `LEFT("INV-2024",3)` → `INV` |\n| `CONCAT` | Gabung teks | `CONCAT(A2," ",B2)` |\n| `VALUE` | Teks → angka | `VALUE("1500")` → `1500` |\n\n**Sebelum → sesudah pada nama TokoSegar:**\n\n| Mentah | Rumus | Bersih |\n|--------|-------|--------|\n| `"  budi  "` | `=PROPER(TRIM(A2))` | `Budi` |\n| `"INV-2024-0007"` | `=RIGHT(A2,4)` | `0007` |\n| `"1.500"` (teks) | `=VALUE(SUBSTITUTE(A2,".",""))` | `1500` |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Alur kerja pembersihan yang berulang\n\n```\nBuat salinan data mentah → jangan edit yang asli\n↓\nHapus duplikat persis → tentukan kolom kunci dulu\n↓\nStandarkan teks → TRIM + PROPER + perbaiki kapitalisasi\n↓\nPerbaiki tipe → angka-teks, tanggal\n↓\nTangani kosong & error → IFERROR/IFNA, atau tandai untuk ditinjau\n↓\nSpot-check → sort, filter, cek kewajaran total\n```\n\n> **Studi kasus mini — ekspor pemasok.** Pemasok mengirim 5.000 baris dengan harga seperti `"Rp 12.500"` sebagai teks, sehingga `SUM` menghasilkan 0. `=VALUE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(A2,"Rp ",""),".",""))` mengonversinya, dan total akhirnya cocok dengan invoice. Satu rumus, satu sore terselamatkan.\n\n---\n\n## Kesalahan umum\n\n- **Mengedit file mentah.** Selalu bersihkan di salinan; simpan asli untuk audit.\n- **Menghapus kosong tanpa diselidiki.** Phone kosong ≠ total order kosong.\n- **TRIM tidak menghapus *semua* spasi** — non-breaking space (char 160) perlu `SUBSTITUTE(A2,CHAR(160),"")`.\n- **Menyembunyikan error yang seharusnya diperbaiki.** `IFERROR(...,0)` bisa diam-diam mengubah bug jadi nol yang salah.\n\n---\n\n## Glosarium\n\n| Istilah | Arti |\n|---------|------|\n| Duplikat | Baris yang mengulang record yang ada |\n| Blank / null | Sel tanpa nilai |\n| Tipe data | Apakah nilai berupa teks, angka, atau tanggal |\n| Fungsi pembungkus | Fungsi seperti IFERROR yang membungkus fungsi lain untuk menangani error |\n| Normalisasi | Membuat format konsisten (kapitalisasi, spasi, satuan) |\n\n---\n\n## Kesimpulan Utama\n\n1. Pembersihan itu sistematis, bukan asal — selalu kerjakan di **salinan** data mentah.\n2. Temukan duplikat dengan **Remove Duplicates** / `COUNTIF`; temukan kosong dengan **Go To Special** / `COUNTBLANK`.\n3. Kenali lima error umum dan bungkus rumus dengan **IFERROR / IFNA** — tapi jangan sembunyikan bug yang harus diperbaiki.\n4. `TRIM`, `PROPER`, `LEFT/RIGHT/MID`, `VALUE` adalah perangkat pembersih harian Anda.\n5. Ikuti alur **salin → dedup → standarkan → perbaiki tipe → tangani kosong → spot-check** setiap kali.\n\n> 💡 Coba exercise matching di bawah, lalu bersihkan dataset utama di tugas.'
WHERE session_number = 'X02';

-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- X03 · Pivot Table untuk Dapatkan Insight
-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
UPDATE public.sessions SET
 content_en = E'## Why pivot tables\n\nA pivot table summarises thousands of rows into insight in seconds — no formulas, no errors, fully re-arrangeable by drag-and-drop. If you can phrase a question as *"total X by Y"*, a pivot answers it faster than any formula.\n\nWe will analyse TokoSegar''s raw sales log.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Anatomy of a pivot table\n\nEvery pivot has four drop zones:\n\n| Zone | Role | TokoSegar example |\n|------|------|-------------------|\n| **Rows** | Categories down the side | Product category |\n| **Columns** | Categories across the top | Month |\n| **Values** | The numbers to aggregate | Sum of revenue |\n| **Filters** | Slice the whole table | Region = Jakarta |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Building your first pivot\n\nRaw log (one row per transaction):\n\n| date | category | region | revenue |\n|------|----------|--------|---------|\n| 2026-03-01 | Produce | Jakarta | 120 |\n| 2026-03-01 | Dairy | Jakarta | 80 |\n| 2026-03-02 | Produce | Bandung | 60 |\n| 2026-03-02 | Produce | Jakarta | 140 |\n\nSteps: select the data → **Insert → PivotTable** → drag `category` to **Rows**, `revenue` to **Values**. Result:\n\n| Category | Sum of revenue |\n|----------|----------------|\n| Produce | 320 |\n| Dairy | 80 |\n| **Grand total** | **400** |\n\nDrag `region` to **Columns** and you get a category × region matrix — same data, new question, zero formulas.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Aggregations and "Show Values As"\n\nDouble-click a value field → **Value Field Settings** to change how numbers combine:\n\n| Aggregation | Answers |\n|-------------|---------|\n| Sum | Total revenue |\n| Count | Number of transactions |\n| Average | Average basket |\n| Max / Min | Biggest / smallest sale |\n\n**Show Values As** reframes the same number:\n\n| Option | Turns revenue into |\n|--------|--------------------|\n| % of Grand Total | Each category''s share of the whole |\n| % of Column Total | Share within each month |\n| Running Total | Cumulative revenue over time |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Advanced moves\n\n- **Grouping**: right-click a date row → **Group** → by Month/Quarter to roll daily data up to months.\n- **Calculated Field**: **PivotTable Analyze → Fields → Calculated Field**, e.g. `profit = revenue - cost`.\n- **Slicers & Timelines**: **Insert → Slicer** gives clickable buttons; **Timeline** gives a date slider — both filter the pivot interactively for a mini dashboard.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Pivot table vs formulas\n\n| Use a pivot when… | Use a formula when… |\n|-------------------|----------------------|\n| Exploring / slicing many ways | You need one specific number in a cell |\n| Grouping and subtotalling | The result must live inside another calculation |\n| The layout should be drag-to-change | The logic must update live with the sheet |\n\n> **Mini case study — category performance.** With one pivot (category in Rows, month in Columns, revenue as Values, region Slicer), TokoSegar found Produce grew 22% in Jakarta but *fell* in Bandung — invisible in the raw log, obvious in the pivot. The fix: shift produce stock toward Jakarta.\n\n---\n\n## Common pitfalls\n\n- **Stale data.** Pivots do not auto-update — **right-click → Refresh** after the source changes.\n- **Source range not expanding.** New rows get ignored unless the source is a Table (`Ctrl+T`) or a dynamic range.\n- **Grouping fails on text dates.** Dates stored as text will not group — convert with `DATEVALUE` first.\n- **Blank cells counted oddly.** Blanks and text in a numeric column force Count instead of Sum.\n\n---\n\n## Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| Pivot table | A drag-and-drop summary of raw rows |\n| Aggregation | How values combine (sum, count, average…) |\n| Calculated field | A new field defined by a formula inside the pivot |\n| Slicer | Clickable filter buttons for a pivot |\n| Grand total | The overall total row/column |\n\n---\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. A pivot answers *"total X by Y"* instantly via four zones: **Rows, Columns, Values, Filters**.\n2. Change the story with **aggregation** (sum/count/avg) and **Show Values As** (% of total, running total).\n3. **Group** dates to months/quarters; add **Calculated Fields**; make it interactive with **Slicers/Timelines**.\n4. Prefer a pivot for exploring and subtotalling; a formula for a single embedded number.\n5. Always **Refresh** after the source changes, and base pivots on a **Table** so new rows are included.\n\n> 💡 Practice with the matching exercises below, then pull insight from the sales dataset in the assignment.',
 content_id = E'## Kenapa pivot table\n\nPivot table meringkas ribuan baris menjadi insight dalam hitungan detik — tanpa rumus, tanpa error, bisa diatur ulang dengan drag-and-drop. Kalau Anda bisa merumuskan pertanyaan sebagai *"total X per Y"*, pivot menjawabnya lebih cepat dari rumus apa pun.\n\nKita akan menganalisis log penjualan mentah TokoSegar.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Anatomi pivot table\n\nSetiap pivot punya empat drop zone:\n\n| Zona | Peran | Contoh TokoSegar |\n|------|-------|------------------|\n| **Rows** | Kategori ke bawah | Kategori produk |\n| **Columns** | Kategori ke samping | Bulan |\n| **Values** | Angka yang diagregasi | Jumlah revenue |\n| **Filters** | Menyaring seluruh tabel | Region = Jakarta |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Membuat pivot pertama Anda\n\nLog mentah (satu baris per transaksi):\n\n| date | category | region | revenue |\n|------|----------|--------|---------|\n| 2026-03-01 | Produce | Jakarta | 120 |\n| 2026-03-01 | Dairy | Jakarta | 80 |\n| 2026-03-02 | Produce | Bandung | 60 |\n| 2026-03-02 | Produce | Jakarta | 140 |\n\nLangkah: pilih data → **Insert → PivotTable** → tarik `category` ke **Rows**, `revenue` ke **Values**. Hasil:\n\n| Kategori | Sum of revenue |\n|----------|----------------|\n| Produce | 320 |\n| Dairy | 80 |\n| **Grand total** | **400** |\n\nTarik `region` ke **Columns** dan Anda dapat matriks kategori × region — data sama, pertanyaan baru, tanpa rumus.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Agregasi dan "Show Values As"\n\nKlik ganda field nilai → **Value Field Settings** untuk mengubah cara angka digabung:\n\n| Agregasi | Menjawab |\n|----------|----------|\n| Sum | Total revenue |\n| Count | Jumlah transaksi |\n| Average | Rata-rata basket |\n| Max / Min | Penjualan terbesar / terkecil |\n\n**Show Values As** membingkai ulang angka yang sama:\n\n| Opsi | Mengubah revenue jadi |\n|------|------------------------|\n| % of Grand Total | Porsi tiap kategori dari keseluruhan |\n| % of Column Total | Porsi dalam tiap bulan |\n| Running Total | Revenue kumulatif seiring waktu |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Jurus tingkat lanjut\n\n- **Grouping**: klik kanan baris tanggal → **Group** → per Bulan/Kuartal untuk menggulung data harian ke bulan.\n- **Calculated Field**: **PivotTable Analyze → Fields → Calculated Field**, mis. `profit = revenue - cost`.\n- **Slicer & Timeline**: **Insert → Slicer** memberi tombol yang bisa diklik; **Timeline** memberi slider tanggal — keduanya menyaring pivot secara interaktif untuk mini dashboard.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Pivot table vs rumus\n\n| Pakai pivot saat… | Pakai rumus saat… |\n|-------------------|--------------------|\n| Mengeksplorasi / mengiris banyak cara | Butuh satu angka spesifik di sel |\n| Mengelompokkan dan subtotal | Hasil harus masuk ke perhitungan lain |\n| Layout ingin diubah drag | Logika harus update live dengan sheet |\n\n> **Studi kasus mini — performa kategori.** Dengan satu pivot (kategori di Rows, bulan di Columns, revenue di Values, Slicer region), TokoSegar menemukan Produce tumbuh 22% di Jakarta tapi *turun* di Bandung — tak terlihat di log mentah, jelas di pivot. Solusi: geser stok produce ke Jakarta.\n\n---\n\n## Kesalahan umum\n\n- **Data basi.** Pivot tidak auto-update — **klik kanan → Refresh** setelah sumber berubah.\n- **Range sumber tidak melebar.** Baris baru terabaikan kecuali sumbernya Table (`Ctrl+T`) atau range dinamis.\n- **Grouping gagal pada tanggal teks.** Tanggal sebagai teks tidak bisa di-group — konversi dengan `DATEVALUE` dulu.\n- **Sel kosong terhitung aneh.** Blank dan teks di kolom numerik memaksa Count alih-alih Sum.\n\n---\n\n## Glosarium\n\n| Istilah | Arti |\n|---------|------|\n| Pivot table | Ringkasan drag-and-drop dari baris mentah |\n| Agregasi | Cara nilai digabung (sum, count, average…) |\n| Calculated field | Field baru yang didefinisikan rumus di dalam pivot |\n| Slicer | Tombol filter yang bisa diklik untuk pivot |\n| Grand total | Baris/kolom total keseluruhan |\n\n---\n\n## Kesimpulan Utama\n\n1. Pivot menjawab *"total X per Y"* seketika lewat empat zona: **Rows, Columns, Values, Filters**.\n2. Ubah cerita dengan **agregasi** (sum/count/avg) dan **Show Values As** (% of total, running total).\n3. **Group** tanggal ke bulan/kuartal; tambah **Calculated Field**; buat interaktif dengan **Slicer/Timeline**.\n4. Utamakan pivot untuk eksplorasi dan subtotal; rumus untuk satu angka tertanam.\n5. Selalu **Refresh** setelah sumber berubah, dan basiskan pivot pada **Table** agar baris baru ikut.\n\n> 💡 Latih dengan exercise matching di bawah, lalu tarik insight dari dataset penjualan di tugas.'
WHERE session_number = 'X03';
